<Window x:Class="EQBuddy.CreatureWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="EQBuddy Kills &amp; Drops" SizeToContent="Height"
        WindowStyle="None" AllowsTransparency="True" Background="Transparent"
        Topmost="True" ShowInTaskbar="False" ResizeMode="CanResize"
        Width="880" WindowStartupLocation="Manual">
    <!-- The KILLS & DROPS theme (docs/Themes.md). David's grouping, 2026-08-20, and it
         corrected mine: I had put Kills into Live Meters with Combat and Healing, and he
         answered "Kills isn't a meter though. we don't track kills per second but we track
         damage per second, healing per second. Kills and Drops should be … Kills and
         Drops ;)" A meter is a per-second board; kills/hour is a rate, which is not the
         same thing.

         BOTH TABS ARE ABOUT THE CREATURE — what died, and what it dropped at what rate.
         That is one question, "is this camp worth it", and it was answered in two places,
         one of them (Drops by creature) buried in the cog menu where nobody found it.

         GearLootWindow is the template, down to the chrome, the 880px landscape width and
         the drag/close/zoom wiring. 880 because the Drops tab is a wide surface — creature
         heading, item name, count and rate on one line, plus a filter row with three export
         buttons — and 520 was the LOOT CARD's width, which is right on the widget (where a
         card shares the monitor with the game) and wrong in a window.

         Why a window and not the overlay: neither tab is a deadline. "What did I kill" and
         "what did it drop" are reviewed, not reacted to, so by CLAUDE.md's surface rule
         neither earns space over the running game. What they earn instead is room to be
         read.

         Nothing below carries a literal size, radius or spacing value — every number
         resolves out of the token dictionary, and DesignRatchetTests fails the build if a
         literal reappears. -->
    <Border Background="{DynamicResource BgBrush}" CornerRadius="{DynamicResource CornerPanel}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource HairlineBrush}" BorderThickness="1"
            MouseLeftButtonDown="OnDrag">
      <Grid>
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
          <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Title row. The icon is a vector (DesignSystem.Icon), never a glyph — emoji
             box outright in the Wine prefixes the Linux and macOS builds run under
             (#148, #166). -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <StackPanel x:Name="TitleRow" Orientation="Horizontal" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
            <Button x:Name="CloseBtn" Style="{StaticResource EqIconButton}" ToolTip="Close"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right" Click="OnClose"/>
        </Grid>

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <!-- Labels, order and keys come from Core's CreatureSurface, and the badges
                 beside them from UI.Shared's CreatureTheme, so this window, the Avalonia
                 widget and EQBuddy Mobile cannot end up naming different tabs or reporting
                 different numbers (#184, #210).

                 WRAPS, and it has to. A horizontal StackPanel measures its children with
                 INFINITE width, so nothing ever reaches a boundary to wrap at and the last
                 chip is simply clipped at the panel's edge — silently, with no ellipsis to
                 say so. That is trap 25, and it cost the Progress theme its Raids tab on
                 the first build. Two chips fit today; the Drops badge is a phrase
                 ("13 creatures"), so they are not fixed-width. -->
            <WrapPanel x:Name="TabStrip" Margin="{DynamicResource StackS}"/>
        </StackPanel>

        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2" x:Name="BodyScroll" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                      Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}">
            <!-- The active tab's surface. It carries no Visibility and no Margin of its
                 own: the lifted control owns both (trap 15 — a control that hides itself
                 inside a host that also hides itself has two switches for one state, and
                 only one of them is ever wired). And neither lifted view brings a
                 ScrollViewer, because THIS is the scroller (trap 36). -->
            <ContentControl x:Name="TabBody"/>
        </ScrollViewer>
        <!-- The mini-dashboard star the Kills card header used to carry.
             It HAD to come somewhere. That star is the only writer MiniStats has for
             "kills", and a fold that drops the last writer of a setting is exactly the
             shape of #204/#209, #210 and #212 (CLAUDE.md trap 20 and trap 26) —
             DeadSettingTests would have failed the build, and before that test existed it
             cost three player-facing bugs. Here it gets the words it never had. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Row="3" x:Name="MiniRow" Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Margin="{DynamicResource PadCard}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
      </Grid>
    </Border>
</Window>
